# HostilePet — environment reference.
#
# TEMPLATE. Copy it to `.env` (git-ignored) and edit that copy:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.env` is loaded twice, and both halves matter.
#
# `electron.vite.config.ts` reads it into the environment of the process that spawns Electron,
# which covers `pnpm dev`. The app also reads the same file itself at startup
# (`src/main/env-file.ts`), because a packaged build has no parent process to inherit from:
# launched from Finder, its environment is whatever launchd gave it. In a bundle the file is
# looked for beside `HostilePet.app` and at `Contents/Resources/.env`; in development it is
# this file, one level up from the package.
#
# An already-exported variable wins over the file, so a one-off run stays a one-off:
#
#     HOSTILEPET_PROVIDER=openai pnpm dev
#
# Editing the file does not reach a running app — nothing re-reads it — so restart:
#
#     pnpm dev                      # development
#     open -a HostilePet            # packaged
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keep every optional line COMMENTED OUT until you set it — `HOSTILEPET_OPENAI_API_KEY` is the
# one exception, because it is read defensively and a blank value means "not set".
#
# Most variables are read defensively, but `HOSTILEPET_MODEL` and
# `HOSTILEPET_OPENAI_BASE_URL` are read with `??`, which does not treat an empty string as
# absent. `HOSTILEPET_MODEL=` therefore selects the real provider and then sends a blank
# model name; the turn fails. Commented-out lines are never exported, so the defaults hold.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The model key. `HOSTILEPET_OPENAI_API_KEY` is read first and the login Keychain is the
# fallback (`src/main/agent/api-key.ts`, `docs/adr/0010-api-key-from-env.md`), so a machine
# that already stores the key there needs nothing here:
#
#     security add-generic-password -s hostilepet.openai -a "$USER" -w
#
# Let `-w` prompt for the value; writing `-w sk-...` puts the key in your shell history.
#
# This file is git-ignored and is the only place a key may sit in plain text. Whichever source
# is used, the key is handed to one call per turn and is never persisted, never logged, and
# never sent to a window. An empty value counts as absent, so the line may be left blank to
# fall back to the Keychain:
#
#HOSTILEPET_OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the model (off by default)
# The model owns every line the pet says, so without this the pet is silent: a real provider is
# only selected when the value is exactly `openai`, AND a key exists in the environment or the
# Keychain, AND the persona artifact is readable. Anything missing leaves the provider that has
# no words in place — the face still changes, nothing is said — and the UI names the reason,
# including which source the key came from (ADR 0011).
#HOSTILEPET_PROVIDER=openai

# Pinned default is `gpt-5.6-luna`. ADR 0006 records that the structured-output smoke test
# has not been run — no key was available when this was wired — so the first real turn is
# the test. Override with a model your key can actually call; nothing downstream depends on
# the name.
#HOSTILEPET_MODEL=gpt-5.6-luna

# Where those calls go. Trailing slashes are stripped.
#HOSTILEPET_OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- runtime artifacts
# Both are runtime data read at startup, never bundled into a source file. These defaults
# are resolved relative to the app directory; set them to point a demo at an edited copy.
#HOSTILEPET_PERSONA=prompts/persona.md
#HOSTILEPET_SITE_CATALOG=packs/site-catalog.json


# ------------------------------------------------------- what reaches the event log
# The browser ticks once a second. That is the transport's resolution, and by itself it
# would write a log line per second per site. The tracker accrues every tick either way —
# this only decides how often that accrual becomes a `site.observed` record, and so how
# often the agent is nudged. Lower it towards 1000 to watch the log fill at the transport's
# own rate; raise it to keep the log sparse while the accrual stays fine.
#HOSTILEPET_OBSERVE_THROTTLE_MS=10000


# -------------------------------------------------------------- when the agent is asked
# An observation opens a settle window, and each further observation restarts it, so a burst
# becomes one call rather than many. This is the clock that sets how often the model is
# actually asked: the browser's 1 s tick travels through the throttle above and lands here,
# where a fast stream is collapsed into a single turn.
#HOSTILEPET_AGENT_SETTLE_MS=20000

# Ceiling on how long a burst may postpone the call. This is what stops a continuously
# browsing person from starving the agent forever: without it, every new event would
# restart the settle window and no call would ever be made. Never lower than the settle
# window; a smaller value is raised to match it.
#HOSTILEPET_AGENT_MAX_WAIT_MS=60000

# The older fixed-clock backstop. It only fires when no burst did.
# Clamped to 30000–60000.
#HOSTILEPET_AGENT_INTERVAL_MS=45000


# ----------------------------------------------------- how long the pet keeps a face
# The four clocks above decide when the agent runs. These two decide how long anyone can
# see that it did — which is the half a person actually watches, and the reason tuning only
# the group above still leaves the pet looking stuck.
#
# How long the pet wears the face a turn earned. It is also the clock that repaints the
# window: when it expires the mood is dropped and the pet goes back to idle. Lower it and
# the face comes and goes faster; the line, if a model wrote one, expires with it.
#HOSTILEPET_PET_LINE_TTL_MS=60000

# How long a page that went silent still counts as being looked at. This is what stops the
# pet pulling a face at a tab nobody is looking at: a closed tab is never announced, so
# presence is what expires in its place. Keep it below `PAGE_STALE_MS` — a lower value is
# raised to it — and below `decayAfterSeconds` (120 s) in `packs/site-catalog.json`.
#HOSTILEPET_PRESENCE_STALE_MS=15000

# How long a silent page keeps its accrued time before it is forgotten. Lowering it lets a
# site fall out of the agent's context sooner, which is what makes the level stop caring
# about a session that has ended. Raised to `HOSTILEPET_PRESENCE_STALE_MS` if set lower.
#HOSTILEPET_PAGE_STALE_MS=90000


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- switches
# Set to exactly `off` to disable the macOS Focus sensor. Anything else — including unset —
# leaves it on. Note that the sensor needs permission before it can read anything, and
# reports `known: false` until then.
#HOSTILEPET_FOCUS_SENSOR=off

# Set to exactly `mock` to run the fake kernel handler. Anything else — including unset —
# selects the real kernel.
#HOSTILEPET_HANDLER=mock

# Demo pacing: set to `1`, `true`, `on` or `yes` and every clock between an observed event
# and the pet's face is lowered together, so one cycle — land on a site, watch the pet
# react, leave, watch it go back to idle — fits in about five seconds:
#
#     observe throttle 1000 · settle 1500 · max wait 2500 · backstop 30000
#     face TTL 5000 · presence 3000 · page 20000
#
# Measured against the real chain, that puts the reaction about 2.5 s after a page opens
# (2.5 s is also the bound while the page keeps ticking, because every tick restarts the
# settle window) and takes the face away again within 5 s of the tab going quiet.
#
# It lowers clocks only. No threshold that decides *whether* the pet reacts moves, so what
# the demo shows is still produced by the real sensor and the real ladder, and Settings
# badges the run as DEMO while it is on (`docs/hackathon.md` §4). Each clock above still
# wins over the preset when it is set explicitly.
#HOSTILEPET_DEMO_FAST=1
